---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import Invite from "../components/Invite.astro";
import { pageTitle } from "../consts";

const description =
	"Akai Milson’s teaching philosophy and instructional pillars for 11th Grade English and AP Seminar: inquiry, research and writing, authentic assessment, and responsible technology.";
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle("Teaching")} description={description}>
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="Teaching"
		title="Creating classrooms where curiosity drives learning"
		lede="Curiosity is a classroom condition. It shows up when the questions are real, the texts can bear pressure, and students trust that revision is part of the work."
	/>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="philosophy-heading">
		<div class="wrap measure prose">
			<h2 id="philosophy-heading">What that sentence means on a weekday</h2>
			<p>
				I do not wait for curiosity to arrive as a personality trait. I build the conditions for it.
				Students get a text, a problem, or a set of sources that will not sit still under a single
				obvious thesis. They get time to notice what is strange before they are asked to prove a
				point. They get models of what careful thinking sounds like, and then they practice it out
				loud and on the page.
			</p>
			<p>
				The teacher’s role is to choose the friction and to coach the next move. A good question is
				specific enough to research and open enough to argue. A good writing conference names one
				place the reasoning slips. A good discussion sends students back to the text with something
				they had not seen.
			</p>
			<p>
				Both 11th Grade English and AP Seminar run on that idea. The products differ. The stance is
				the same: students are thinkers first, and the assignment is a place to show the thinking.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section band-warm" aria-labelledby="pillars-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Instructional pillars</p>
				<h2 id="pillars-heading">Four commitments that organize the courses</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="stack">
				<article class="panel">
					<p class="kicker">01</p>
					<h3>Inquiry-Driven Instruction</h3>
					<p>
						Units begin with a question students can own. In English, that might be how a writer
						builds a narrator the reader should not fully trust. In AP Seminar, it might be how a
						community defines a problem differently depending on who is asked. Students learn to
						narrow, to define terms, and to explain why the question is worth a reader’s time.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Questions are drafted, tested, and revised before a thesis is allowed to harden.</li>
						<li>Discussion is a research tool, not a break from the “real” work of writing.</li>
						<li>Students track how their question changed and what evidence forced the change.</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="panel" id="research-writing">
					<p class="kicker">02</p>
					<h3>Research &amp; Writing Mastery</h3>
					<p>
						Confident communicators can hold a line of reasoning. They choose evidence that actually
						supports the point, and they write the commentary that tells a reader how. Research and
						writing are one practice here: you cannot write past a source you have not understood,
						and you have not understood it if you cannot use it precisely.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Paragraphs are built as claim, evidence, and commentary that earns the evidence.</li>
						<li>Students distinguish summary from analysis, and quotation from proof.</li>
						<li>Style work is attached to meaning: sentence control in the service of an idea.</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						The <a href="/resources#english">11th grade writing tools</a> and the
						<a href="/resources#ap-seminar">AP Seminar organizers</a> are the scaffolds for this pillar.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="panel">
					<p class="kicker">03</p>
					<h3>Authentic Assessment</h3>
					<p>
						Students should be assessed on work that resembles the thinking the course claims to
						teach. A literary argument, a researched report, a team presentation, and a reflection
						on how a draft changed are closer to the real task than a quiz about vocabulary in
						isolation. Rubrics describe the thinking, and students see them early enough to aim.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Major tasks are previewed with models, criteria, and a revision window.</li>
						<li>Peer review uses a protocol so feedback is specific and kind.</li>
						<li>Reflection asks what changed in the idea, not only what changed in the grammar.</li>
					</ul>
				</article>

				<article class="panel">
					<p class="kicker">04</p>
					<h3>Responsible AI &amp; Digital Literacy</h3>
					<p>
						Students already meet fluent, uncredited text online. Pretending otherwise does not
						teach judgment. We study how to evaluate sources, how to read laterally, and how to use
						digital tools, including generative AI, without handing over authorship. Technology
						should enhance learning. It does not replace the student as the thinker.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Source evaluation is practiced on live claims, not only on pre-selected packets.</li>
						<li>AI use is disclosed, bounded, and followed by verification.</li>
						<li>Privacy is part of the norm: other people’s drafts are not pasted into a tool.</li>
					</ul>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" id="courses" aria-labelledby="courses-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Course highlights</p>
				<h2 id="courses-heading">Two courses, one standard for thinking</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="split-even">
				<article class="panel">
					<p class="kicker">11th Grade English</p>
					<h3>Literature, rhetoric, and a writing life</h3>
					<p>
						Eleventh grade English is where students learn to stay with a complicated text and to
						write arguments that sound like they have actually read it. We move between literary
						analysis, rhetorical reading, discussion, and shorter inquiries that build research
						habits before a longer paper.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Close reading that notices pattern, perspective, and purpose.</li>
						<li>Academic paragraphs and full essays with commentary, not plot summary.</li>
						<li>Discussion routines that produce a better draft the next day.</li>
						<li>Peer review and conferences focused on the line of reasoning.</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						<a class="text-link" href="/resources#english">Classroom writing resources</a>
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="panel">
					<p class="kicker">AP Seminar</p>
					<h3>College-level inquiry, in a team and alone</h3>
					<p>
						AP Seminar, part of the AP Capstone experience, asks students to investigate real-world
						issues from multiple perspectives and to communicate an argument in writing and in a
						presentation. The course is demanding because the thinking is real, not because the
						directions are mysterious.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>The QUEST cycle as a working method, not a poster.</li>
						<li>Individual and team performance tasks with scaffolds that fade.</li>
						<li>Stimulus materials read for conversation and tension.</li>
						<li>Presentations rehearsed for clarity, evidence, and the questions that follow.</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						<a class="text-link" href="/ap-seminar">A deeper look at AP Seminar</a>
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section band-warm">
		<div class="wrap measure prose">
			<h2>A rhythm students can count on</h2>
			<p>
				A typical week mixes reading, a short writing move, and talk. Students know when they are
				gathering, when they are drafting, and when they are revising. Homework is purposeful.
				Class time is where the hard part happens with a teacher nearby: choosing evidence,
				repairing a transition, figuring out what a source is not saying.
			</p>
			<p>
				I would rather students attempt a precise claim and revise it than hide inside a safe
				summary. The grade follows the thinking they can show, including the thinking they improved.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<Invite
		title="Want the classroom version of this philosophy?"
		text="The resource library is free to use with attribution. If you want a conversation about your own department, start there or write directly."
	/>
</BaseLayout>
